  <section xml:id="sec-course-overview">
  <title>Course Overview</title>

  <introduction>
    <image source="usabanner.jpg">
<shortdescription>decorative banner image</shortdescription>
</image>
  </introduction>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>👋 Welcome!</title>

    <p>
      I'm your instructor, Steven Clontz. I prefer to be addressed as "Dr./Prof.
      Clontz" or "Dr./Prof. C", and use "he/him" pronouns.
    </p>
    <image source="headshot.png" width="50%"/>
    <p>
      My title here at South is "Professor of Mathematics", which means I'm a
      tenured research mathematician, responsible for teaching courses to
      students like you, as well as publishing papers that prove new
      mathematical facts. My scholarly interests include topology (the area of
      math that explains how you visually recognize different shaped fonts as
      the same letters), game theory (the area of math that explains how to win
      games as optimally as possible), sociotechnical infrastructure and
      human-computer interaction in mathematics (areas of computer science that
      study how humans use technology to collaborate on mathematical problems),
      and mathematics education and outreach (a.k.a. how to make math engaging
      and approachable by general audiences).
    </p>

    <p>
      Fun fact: I'm a huge Nintendo 🎮 and Disney 🏰 nerd. My eight-year-old got
      As in math last year, but I'm secretly more excited that she's gotten deep
      into the lore of Deltarune (not the Weird Route, obvs). If you need to
      distract me, get me talking about Super Nintendo World, which I finally
      got to visit last summer (but failed to beat any minigames).
    </p>

    <p>
      If you are interested in any combination of math, games, puzzles,
      computers, and/or education, I'm always interested in finding ways to
      support student projects. Just let me know!
    </p>

    <note xml:id="fine-print">
      <title>⚖ For those who read the fine print</title>

      <p>
        I wrote this page as a casual and unofficial overview of my intentions
        for our course this semester. It provides context and motivation for,
        but doesn't replace, our official course syllabus.
      </p>

      <p>
        The university's Additional Academic Course Policies also apply:
        <TODO type="unknown-inline-type"><!--todo: unknown inline type "link"--><c>&#x3C;https://www.southalabama.edu/departments/academicaffairs/resources/policies/additionalacademiccoursepolicies.pdf></c></TODO>
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      Our class meets Mon/Wed/Fri at either 9:05am-9:55am (section 104) or
      10:10am-11:00am (section 101) in MSPB 360.
    </p>

    <p>
      I offer drop-in student office hours in my office MSPB 314 from
      12:00noon-2:00pm on Mon/Wed/Fri. Note the "drop-in" means no appointment
      is required, and the "student" means <alert>YOU</alert>: this is the time
      I've explicitly set aside to support you!
    </p>

    <p>
      If you need to contact me, don't hesitate to send me a message via Canvas,
      or even better, if it's about something you've submitted in Canvas, leave
      a comment on your Canvas submission.
    </p>

    <note xml:id="student-quotes">
      <title>💬 What students say about this course</title>

      <p>
        "I enjoyed getting a more hands-on approach to math and getting to write
        on a white board. I also enjoyed the classroom environment that he
        created. I felt comfortable making mistakes and not getting things right
        the first time. I struggle a lot with anxiety, but his class was one I
        didn't worry as much in. Not because I felt like it didn't matter, but
        because it was truly a place I could come into and truly learn instead
        of trying to be perfect all the time."
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>🧮 What are we learning this semester?</title>

    <p>
      Our course this semester is an introduction to linear algebra. This branch
      of mathematics is focused on vector spaces (collections of vectors for
      which addition and multiplication is defined) and linear maps (rules for
      transforming vector spaces that preserve addition and multiplication).
    </p>

    <p>
      Linear algebra provides the tools for converting many kinds of data from
      one format to another without losing its linear structure. For example,
      linear algebra explains how to transform a 3D model into a 2D image in a
      sensible way:
    </p>
    <image source="teapot.png"/>
    <p>
      This course will be structured around five big questions, each of which is
      split into 4 to 7 learning outcomes. Don't worry if you don't know what
      all these words mean just yet - we have a semester to get it all figured
      out.
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>💡 Big Questions and Learning Outcomes</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              How can we solve systems of linear equations? By the end of this
              course, you should be able to...
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Translate back and forth between a system of linear
                    equations, a vector equation, and the corresponding
                    augmented matrix.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Explain why a matrix isn't in reduced row echelon form, and
                    put a matrix in reduced row echelon form.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine the number of solutions for a system of linear
                    equations or a vector equation.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Compute the solution set for a system of linear equations or
                    a vector equation with infinitely many solutions.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What is a space of Euclidean vectors? By the end of this course,
              you should be able to...
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a Euclidean vector can be written as a linear
                    combination of a given set of Euclidean vectors by solving
                    an appropriate vector equation.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a set of Euclidean vectors spans Rn by solving
                    appropriate vector equations.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a subset of Rn is a subspace or not.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a set of Euclidean vectors is linearly
                    dependent or independent by solving an appropriate vector
                    equation.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Explain why a set of Euclidean vectors is or is not a basis
                    of Rn.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Compute a basis for the subspace spanned by a given set of
                    Euclidean vectors, and determine the dimension of the
                    subspace.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Find a basis for the solution set of a homogeneous system of
                    equations.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              How can we understand linear maps algebraically? By the end of
              this course, you should be able to...
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a map between Euclidean vector spaces is linear
                    or not.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Translate back and forth between a linear transformation of
                    Euclidean spaces and its standard matrix, and perform
                    related computations.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Compute a basis for the kernel and a basis for the image of
                    a linear map, and verify that the rank-nullity theorem holds
                    for a given linear map.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a given linear map is injective and/or
                    surjective.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Explain why a given set with defined addition and scalar
                    multiplication does satisfy a given vector space property,
                    but nonetheless isn't a vector space.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Answer questions about vector spaces of polynomials or
                    matrices.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What algebraic structure do matrices have? By the end of this
              course, you should be able to...
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Multiply matrices.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Determine if a matrix is invertible, and if so, compute its
                    inverse and use it to solve an appropriate system of
                    equations.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Calculate the change-of-basis matrix for the standard basis
                    to a non-standard basis of Euclidean space.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Express row operations through matrix multiplication.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              How do we understand linear maps geometrically? By the end of this
              course, you should be able to...
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Describe how a row operation affects the determinant of a
                    matrix.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Compute the determinant of a matrix.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Find the eigenvalues of a matrix.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Find a basis for the eigenspace of a matrix associated with
                    a given eigenvalue.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>📚 Textbook and Resources</title>

    <p>
      The textbook David Austin's Understanding Linear Algebra is an excellent
      free and open source reference text that is available to you
      electronically. However, if you want to support the author, hard copies
      are available for purchase.
      <TODO type="unknown-inline-type"><!--todo: unknown inline type "link"--><c>&#x3C;https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/home.html></c></TODO>
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>💸 Don't pay for what you don't need!</title>

      <p>
        All materials necessary to complete course requirements will be provided
        to you at no cost via Canvas, and participation in the university's
        JagPack program is not required or expected for this course. For
        information on JagPack, including information on how to opt out, check
        out this page.
        <TODO type="unknown-inline-type"><!--todo: unknown inline type "link"--><c>&#x3C;https://www.southalabama.edu/programs/jagpack></c></TODO>
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      That said, you'll be spending most of your time using resources from the
      free and open-source Linear Algebra for Team-Based Inquiry Learning
      project. This is a product of my National Science Foundation sponsored
      project studying how to creating activities that help students learn
      linear algebra together, and train faculty to use those activities in the
      classroom. As such, you get to use the bleeding-edge "preview" edition of
      these materials, which are subject to change. This just means that I don't
      encourage you to print anything out; if anything drastic changes after we
      get to it in class, I'll give you a heads up, but things in future
      chapters may be changed online without notice.
      <TODO type="unknown-inline-type"><!--todo: unknown inline type "link"--><c>&#x3C;https://tbil.org></c></TODO>
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>💻 Technology</title>

    <p>
      I'm a huge advocate for using technology to help with learning and doing
      mathematics. The main tool I recommend for this class is SageCell, a
      website that runs code written in Python, SageMath, Octave, and more.
      <TODO type="unknown-inline-type"><!--todo: unknown inline type "link"--><c>&#x3C;https://sagecell.sagemath.org></c></TODO>
    </p>

    <p>
      Of course, what about AI? 🤖 That's its own can of worms for so many
      reasons, but the short version is that the goal of this class is for you
      to learn math, not to learn how to pretend you know math by parroting what
      a computer tells you to say. I'm setting you up for success to learn
      things for yourself, so use AI tools sparingly, if at all.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>👥 Working Together</title>

    <p>
      You'll be grouped into teams early in the semester, and you will be
      working in teams during most of our time spent in our class meetings. But
      if you don't like team projects, don't worry: your letter grade in this
      course depends only upon your individual learning of the course material.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before each new Big Question, you'll complete an individual readiness
      quiz, followed by a team readiness quiz.
    </p>

    <p>
      We'll then explore each relevant Learning Outcome together in class over
      the course of three-ish weeks. I'll introduce each topic and serve as your
      guide as you work through team activities that will help you understand
      each topic and prepare you to answer relevant questions.
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>🙌 Attendance</title>

      <p>
        To ensure all teams are engaged in learning the course material, regular
        in-person attendance is necessary.
      </p>

      <p>
        All students are allowed five class absences for any reason. After that
        a grade penalty will be applied, unless you meet with me during
        scheduled office hours to discuss the absences and make a plan for
        re-engagement in the course. There's no official designation of
        "excused" or "unexcused" absences, but if your absences are "excusable"
        (sickness, interviews, university events) this will be a very quick and
        easy conversation!
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      One important point: at the end of the day I need to see what you've
      learned. If you get help on a submission from a classmate or a technology
      like generative AI, make it clear that you did so in your submission, or
      you risk being liable for plagiarism and an academic dishonesty
      accusation. If you make it clear, I still need to have enough information
      to know that you personally understand the relevant learning outcome.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>💬 Practice and Quizzes</title>

    <p>
      Your primary goal this semester is to show me you understand most, if not
      all, of our twenty-five Learning Outcomes. Each Learning Outcome has
      several Practice Exercises and a Quiz associated with it in Canvas.
    </p>

    <p>
      (TODO multiple choice...)
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>📆 Due Dates and Deadlines</title>

    <p>
      Anything I assign for you to do in this class will be assigned in Canvas.
      Canvas allows me to set "Due Dates" and "Deadlines".
    </p>

    <p>
      The Due Date marks when the assignment is positioned in your Canvas
      calendar, and any submission received after the Due Date is marked as
      "Late". There's no penalty for being "Late"; however, my time to help
      students is finite, and if I have to make a choice, I'll spend that time
      on the students with fewer "Late" submissions than those with more.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Deadline marks when the assignment can no longer be submitted in
      Canvas at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that some assignments must be submitted before other assignments may
      be attempted. So watch out - an assignment without a Deadline may be
      blocking another assignment that does have a Deadline.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>💡 Course Reflections</title>

    <p>
      Students will meet with the instructor in his office for up to three
      in-person Course Reflections throughout the semester. Each reflection will
      be a conversation about the course material covered to that point in the
      semester.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>📒 Exams</title>

    <p>
      This course includes a comprehensive Final Exam, given at the time
      scheduled by the university.
    </p>

    <p>
      💡 Final Course Reflection At the end of the semester, I will ask you to
      complete a Course Reflection looking back over your progress course, and
      provide evidence for any learning outcomes you still need to demonstrate
      that you should "✔️ Meet Expectations" for.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Final Course Reflection will be worth up to 3 additional ✔️s, beyond
      the 25 possible for Learning Outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
      🆎 Assigning Final Letter Grades According to the specifications I outline
      in the course syllabus, here's what you need to do to get a good final
      letter grade in this course:
    </p>

    <p>
      Your first priority is earning ✔️s: by the end of the semester you need 20
      for a C, and 26 for an A. To earn a passing grade, you must pass either
      the Midterm Exam or Final Exam. To earn a grade of A or B, you must pass
      the Final Exam, and you must also submit a Final Course Reflection. 🚀
      Let's Go! That's it! Remember, the syllabus has all the official rules and
      regulations for the course, but I hope this document helps break it down
      for everyone. Let me know if there's anything about the course overall
      that you think I should add here.
    </p>

    <p>
      One other thing: I mentioned everything I want you to do this semester
      will be assigned in Canvas. That means you can keep up with your
      assignment Due Dates by looking at the Canvas Calendar, looking at the
      Course Summary at the bottom of the syllabus page, and the "To Do" sidebar
      on the Canvas homepage for this course. Why not head there now and see
      what's coming up?
    </p>
  </subsubsection>
</section>
</article>
</pretext>